    tripwireNeg -- THE UNION TRIPWIRE'S NEGATIVE CONTROL. Owed since SEQ 120,
    built 2026-09-02 under SEQ 130.

    THE SEAL'S OWN WORDS, 2026-09-01f: "The refusal has NO NEGATIVE CONTROL -- it
    has never been made to fire, so 'it stays silent' is not yet evidence that it
    CAN speak." This file is that evidence.

    HOW IT FIRES. The tripwire refuses when the callee's `argument` attribute body
    holds a non-zero union that is NOT a group -- foreign data whose bytes would
    merely read as a pointer. An ordinary action's argument attribute holds
    nothing, which is why the alarm has been silent for its whole life. tnLoaded
    declares `argument=42`, so its argument attribute body carries a NUMBER before
    any bind happens, and the channel must refuse by name rather than clobber it.

    TWO ROWS, AND THE PAIR IS THE POINT. tnPlain must NOT refuse and tnLoaded
    MUST -- an alarm that fires on everything is as useless as one that fires on
    nothing. Rule H7: this row certifies the refusal only because its sibling
    stays silent under the identical call.

    ⚠ ONLY MEANINGFUL UNDER gNoUnwrap = 1. With the flip off the channel arm is
    not reached at all and BOTH rows are silent, which asserts nothing. Run it
    flipped or do not run it.
